{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner read the complete N4 design at request/act-guidance 3973f302e82fe91927f187a8fbb3b7dd9a19eec1, its complete original request 5fee04f4 and effective builder promise bc530039. Targeted primary-source reads at d691e6e9 covered the complete contract errors.ts, R-ADM-1/3/8/9 and R-IDEM, R-EVAL-5 and R-DECL-16/17, client act/replay and named retention, complete MCP server.ts and toolsets.ts, policy rule validation, delegation guards, last-admin guard, and actual catalogue/read/tool shapes. These are source observations, not a new runtime probe, source-verifier verdict, design adoption, or implementation commission.\n\nProposed defaults for the five choices in section 11:\n1. Text only for this delivery. Preserve the Room's structured result/refusal/error unchanged and show its words first. Short supplemental text, including recorded IDs and available evidence, belongs in the existing surfaces. No new guidance field or tool/schema amendment.\n2. Prefer one transport-neutral public client module for outcome guidance. It concerns client operation context and recovery, not policy evaluation. Keep declaration/meaning/fields ownership in policy and actual eligibility ownership in the existing MCP authorization predicate. Name a proposed reviewed public export, not an export claimed to exist. CLI, MCP and C5 may format the same explanation without copying a second decision table.\n3. Show one short hint by default where an actual authorized caller snapshot and catalogue are already available; otherwise say that caller authority is unknown and show only the declaration. Use the grantor's member role for a delegated caller, implicit admin authority, who.delegable and exact active grant bindings. Tool hiding and token possession are not permission. Do not invent role/grant facts from a bare bearer token or assume that an acts call already returns them. Specify the existing host context passed into the call formatter: server.ts currently obtains caller only for tools/list, while tools/call goes directly to callTool. Internal use of existing authorized inputs is a design implementation choice, not a newly delivered Room read.\n4. Do not commission section 8's optional caller object now. Retain it as a possible later sketch with N4/library/contract owners and an observed cold-run need as trigger. It is not a complete reviewed contract proposal merely because one object shape is printed. Any actual new authoritative read still requires the exact separately reviewed contract and transport/authority decisions; none is silently adopted here.\n5. Use the current starter desk as the proposed coding example, pinned to 6c7b42d0, with its legitimate code-review grant and independent reviewer. This chooses no N3 completion or deployment claim. If Jam is the first available qualifying application, retain that alternative and record which exact acts were unfamiliar. N4's later acceptance can share a legitimate N3/cold witness where it really meets each condition. Neither N3 nor N4 becomes a first-Jam prerequisite.\n\nRepair these planning gaps before formal review:\nA. Outcome certainty and operation kind must precede classification by error name. A refusal is a known domain outcome. In particular policy-budget-exceeded and policy-type-error are deterministic recorded outcomes under R-EVAL-5, not unknown admission. Exact replay returns that refusal; fixing policy does not make the old recorded request succeed. A deliberate later attempt is distinct from settling the original. policy-runtime during admission records nothing, while notification failure occurs after commitment. A timeout of an attention read or an operation wait is not an unknown act admission and must not tell the caller to repeat a write. Respect retryable, maybeRecorded, retryAfterMs, actual call/transport phase and any retained receipt/operation; do not call every timeout/unavailable/internal/rate-limit outcome a possibly recorded act from the code alone. For genuinely uncertain act delivery preserve the original prepared intent and settlement. Read tools also have structured not-found values; do not turn them into ArtroomError or treat catalogue absence/tool omission as proof of authority.\n\nB. A single fixed rule-to-situation table cannot prove all the proposed classifications from the returned shape alone. Refusal.rule is PlatformRule | RuleId; current policy validation reserves only admin-approval, so application policy IDs can collide with strings such as not-holder or obligation-open. Refusal has no source discriminator. This is a source-qualified ambiguity, not a commissioned defect or new protocol gate. When provenance cannot be established from already available authoritative evidence, show the Room's reason/fix and an explicitly unclassified explanation. Do not parse arbitrary wording into an authoritative category, or call every unknown policy ID software newer than the guide. A separately proposed source discriminator is not adopted by this direction.\n\nC. Keep complete rule/code inventory, but make the table a qualified explanation and next-evidence table rather than seven unconditional truths. last-admin is an authorized roster operation blocked by present state; the source's fix is to make another member an admin, not to grant the caller a new role. delegation-invalid has more causes than the two listed: missing/wrong grantee or lane/kind, nondelegable kind, old binding, expired/revoked grant, inactive grantor key/member, lost grantor role and prohibited roster delegation. Preserve actual reason/fix and do not assume that the old grantor can always repair it. not-authorized-reviewer/checker and self-review concern this subject as well as actor authority. reserved, objection-open, obligation-open, lane-held and scope-overlap need qualified next steps: a reservation can name this member; a caller may qualify to review/check, withdraw an objection, take another allowed path, or wait for a deadline. Do not assert that nothing the caller sends can help unless the current evidence establishes it. A guidance step can be useful without promising its next act will be admitted.\n\nD. Separate exact recovery from new intent everywhere, including the short guide. Client generic act() prepares anew; replay() sends retained PreparedAct unchanged. A direct signer persists onPrepared before send and resumes with replay, with Room/actor/credential/operation/binding/idempotency context under its existing owner. For bearer MCP the contract permits the same unchanged call under the same still-usable session, with its exact binding and key; credential expiry/revocation leaves the original outcome unresolved, not permission to move it to a fresh token/signer/key. Do not imply that text guidance adds cross-process durability to the MCP core or implements C3/C5/N3 stores. After a definite unrecorded binding-stale, R-DECL-16 permits caller-authorized same-intent signing under the new binding with the same idempotency key. A stricter new-key application convention may be labelled; it is not a universal contract rule. After a genuinely unknown outcome never silently rebind, replace credentials or invent a new intent.\n\nE. Recorded status comes from the actual returned act ID and the contract path, not a guessed category. R-ADM-8 includes a refused join exception at steps 7–9; idempotency-mismatch records no new entry; policy evaluation refusals can be recorded. An absent act field does not identify which of every conceivable client/transport failures occurred. Keep refusal entries, accepted record, landing/workspace operation and publication results distinct, including the same operation/unknown outcome in agent and browser. Point to the actual candidate actsAt/MCP acts(at or policy), catalogue fields and existing lane/proposal/operation/explain inputs. fieldsOf is a public policy helper, not a new MCP read.\n\nF. Repair the bounded cold scenario so it actually covers the original absent, forbidden, state-blocked and externally waiting paths as well as deliberate changed-meaning handling. The current six-step table has no explicit state-blocked case. Give the agent an explicit grant for the code acts needed to propose and land, with accept excluded; answer-only authority does not supply that coding path. Declare the relevant question entry/thread/lane targets accurately, and arrange the missing-object case against the actual returned shape, not an assumed refusal. On a changed answer binding under an old grant, describe the actual authority/binding order and the later need for deliberate grantor action. Finish the useful result through the independently controlled reviewer and holder acceptance. Record agreement as correct explanation and appropriate recovery subject to state/authority changes, not a guaranteed admitted next write.\n\nG. Retain the original one bounded cold-agent acceptance and its turns/tokens/help/refusals/authoritative results. Do not add a mandatory second complete cold run merely to compare text. A small targeted control may investigate whether guidance helps, with its actual scope and uncertainty stated; a nondifference between two stochastic agent runs is not proof of uselessness. Reuse the existing lost-result/revocation witnesses and any legitimate shared starter/Jam run. Replace G1's tests that simply mirror each table row with meaningful focused controls at changed boundaries: known refusal versus unknown write versus wait timeout, ambiguous rule provenance, current authority versus target-dependent waiting, and retained intent versus deliberate replacement. Completeness of a fixed inventory can be a focused static consistency check. No guard sweep, per-field inventory, numerical test quota, repeated platform suite or root gate for this prose is requested.\n\nH. Repository-authored labels/help and refusal reason/fix remain attributed task data. The short guide should say to assess Next against the person's task and current authority, rather than blindly follow text as new authority. Preserve links to the original declaration/policy and recorded outcome. Manual anchors stay N6's future output; runtime text must be understandable before those anchors exist. C5 retains the full hosted human journey; this note can give it a common explanation boundary without claiming delivery of it.\n\nThese are proposed design defaults and current-source corrections within original N4, not independent guarded approval or adoption. Keep original complete N4 implementation/acceptance, the four situations, Stage5/MCP source review, full N6 manual and C1–C6/N3/N5 scopes. Builder implementation follows exact independent design review and adoption under a separate commission. Highest test-overhead priority, acts/actual Jam blockers and builder's first-task readiness order remain unchanged. No test, install, provider action, new reviewer or source-repair restart was performed.","body":{"head":"3973f302e82fe91927f187a8fbb3b7dd9a19eec1","no_git_artifact":"true","scope":"N4 proposed defaults and complete draft read with targeted source observations; repair design before review, no runtime commission","source_approval":"none"}}